0:{"rsc":["$","$1","c",{"children":[[["$","$L2","4.8",{"topic":{"id":"4.8","title":"Network Traffic Analysis using DEFT Tools","minutes":13,"summary":"Use packet and flow utilities to answer defined questions, preserving capture context and avoiding unsupported content or identity claims.","sections":[{"title":"Prepare the traffic dataset","body":["A supplied PCAP or PCAPNG can be examined with Wireshark or command-line utilities such as tcpdump; Zeek can produce structured connection summaries. DEFT package availability varies by release. Record the actual tool and version, input hash, capture interface if known, filters, and time settings. A display filter changes what is shown, not necessarily what the original capture contains.","Start with endpoints, protocols, capture duration, and missing-packet indicators. Understand whether the capture came from a host, span port, tap, or proxy. Each observation point sees a different part of the path. Checksum offloading can create apparent errors on a local capture; investigate before treating them as evidence of corruption."]},{"title":"Follow streams and reconstruct events","body":["Conversation filters and stream following can make a protocol exchange readable, but exported objects are new derived files. Hash and identify them separately. Encrypted traffic may expose metadata without content. Do not bypass encryption or infer a page’s contents from a domain name alone. Preserve the relevant packet numbers and original capture.","For DNS, compare queries, responses, transaction identifiers, and timing. For HTTP, examine method, host, path, status, and content type where available. For periodic connections, measure intervals and variation. A useful finding explains what was observed and whether a normal service could produce the same pattern."]},{"title":"Report beyond the tool screenshot","body":["A screenshot of a packet list is not a complete analysis. State the filter, time basis, endpoints, relevant packets, interpretation, and limitations. If NAT or DHCP prevents device attribution, say so and identify the lawful records needed. Avoid publishing unrelated payloads or personal information in a teaching report.","Export only necessary derived artifacts and document their relationship to the capture. Where a second tool confirms a finding, explain what it checked. The course’s network lab uses synthetic records and never scans or contacts the displayed addresses. This keeps the exercise reproducible and safe."]}],"practice":{"title":"Explain the capture boundary","scenario":"A synthetic capture shows periodic connections from a NAT address to a shared hosting endpoint.","steps":["Record the observation point and filter.","Measure the intervals and compare payload sizes.","Identify why the public address may represent several devices.","Write a source-linked finding and request only the records needed for attribution."],"debrief":"Traffic analysis can establish a communication pattern while leaving device and human attribution unresolved."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["it-act","bsa","bnss"],"sources":["cert","nist-guide","mitre"],"labId":"network","unit":4,"objectives":["Explain prepare the traffic dataset and apply it to an investigation.","Explain follow streams and reconstruct events and apply it to an investigation.","Explain report beyond the tool screenshot and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-4.8","prompt":"What does a display filter do?","options":["Deletes all other packets from the original capture.","Changes which packets are displayed for analysis.","Decrypts every protocol.","Identifies the human sender."],"answer":1,"explanation":"A display filter controls the view. The underlying capture should remain preserved and unchanged.","unit":4}}}]],["$L3"],"$L4"]}],"isPartial":false,"staleTime":300,"varyParams":null,"buildId":"mMiCr23A_-Cpk0hZkfT7X"}
